WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
When you implement backpropagation for your neural network, you need to really compute

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.040
the slope or the derivative of the activation functions.

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:13.360
So let's take a look at our choices of activation functions and how you can compute the slope

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:14.360
of these functions.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:18.520
Here's the familiar sigmoid activation function.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:23.640
And so for any given value of z, maybe this value of z, this function will have some

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:29.200
slope or some derivative corresponding to, if you draw a little line there, you know,

00:00:29.200 --> 00:00:32.840
the height over width of this little triangle here.

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:41.840
So if g of z is the sigmoid function, then the slope of the function is d dz g of z.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:48.760
And so we know from calculus that this is the slope of g of x at z.

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:54.440
And if you are familiar with calculus and know how to take derivatives, if you take the derivative

00:00:54.440 --> 00:01:00.120
of the sigmoid function, it is possible to show that it is equal to this formula.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:03.260
And again, I'm not going to do the calculus steps.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.380
But if you're familiar with calculus, feel free to pause the video and try to prove this

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:08.480
yourself.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:16.880
And so this is equal to just g of z times 1 minus g of z.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.800
So let's just sanity check that this expression makes sense.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:29.800
First, if z is very large, let's say z is equal to 10, then g of z will be close to

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:30.800
1.

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:38.120
And so the formula we have on the left tells us that d dz g of z does be close to g of

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:46.640
z, which is equal to 1 times 1 minus 1, which is therefore very close to 0.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.600
But this isn't correct because when z is very large, the slope is close to 0.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:57.840
Conversely, if z is equal to minus 10, so it says way out there, then g of z is close

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:59.640
to 0.

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:05.400
So the formula on the left tells us d dz g of z will be close to g of z, which is 0

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:06.880
times 1 minus 0.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:11.000
And so it is also very close to 0, which is correct.

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:17.360
Finally, if z is equal to 0, then g of z is equal to 1 half.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.560
That's the sigmoid function right here.

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:27.880
And so the derivative is equal to 1 half times 1 minus 1 half, which is equal to

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:28.640
1 quarter.

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.160
And that actually turns out to be the correct value of the derivative or the

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:36.120
slope of this function when z is equal to 0.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.880
Finally, just to introduce one more piece of notation, sometimes instead of

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.440
writing this thing, the shorthand for the derivative is g prime of z.

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:52.160
So g prime of z in calculus, the little dash on top is called prime.

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:56.680
But so g prime of z is the shorthand for the calculus for the derivative of the

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.760
function of g with respect to the input variable z.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:10.640
And then in a neural network, we have a equals g of z, right?

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:13.280
So g prime of z equals this.

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.840
Then this formula also simplifies to a times 1 minus a.

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:23.480
So sometimes the implementation, you might see something like g prime of z equals

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:25.480
a times 1 minus a.

00:03:25.480 --> 00:03:30.240
And that just refers to the observation that g prime,

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:33.520
which just means the derivative, is equal to this over here.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:38.200
And the advantage of this formula is that if you've already computed the value

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:43.120
for a, then by using this expression, you can very quickly compute the value for

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:44.800
the slope for g prime as well.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:48.120
All right, so that was the sigmoid activation function.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.200
Let's now look at the tanh activation function.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:59.600
Similar to what we had previously, the definition of d dz g of z is the slope

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.400
of g of z at a particular point of z.

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:11.240
And if you look at the formula for the hyperbolic tangent function, and if you

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:20.540
know calculus, you can take derivatives and show that this simplifies to this formula.

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:27.300
And using the shorthand we had previously, we're going to call this g prime of z again.

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:30.700
So if you want, you can sanity check that this formula makes sense.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:37.940
So for example, if z is equal to 10, tanh of z would be very close to 1.

00:04:37.940 --> 00:04:42.700
This goes from plus 1 to minus 1.

00:04:42.700 --> 00:04:48.300
And then g prime of z, according to this formula, will be about 1 minus 1 squared.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:50.340
So derivative equals 0.

00:04:50.340 --> 00:04:53.940
So that was if z is very large, the slope is close to 0.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.060
Conversely, if z is very small, say z is equal to minus 10,

00:04:59.060 --> 00:05:02.860
then tanh of z will be close to minus 1.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:08.940
And so g prime of z will be close to 1 minus negative 1 squared.

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.900
So it's close to 1 minus 1, which is also close to 0.

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:19.100
And then finally, if z is equal to 0, then tanh of z is equal to 0.

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:25.740
And then the slope is actually equal to 1, which is actually the slope when z is equal to 0.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:32.940
So just to summarize, if a is equal to g of z, so if a is equal to this tanh of z,

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:38.540
then the derivative g prime of z is equal to 1 minus a squared.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:41.940
So once again, if you've already computed the value of a,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:46.380
you can use this formula to very quickly compute the derivative as well.

00:05:46.380 --> 00:05:51.780
Finally, here's how you compute the derivatives for the ReLU and Leakey ReLU activation functions.

00:05:51.780 --> 00:05:58.020
For the ReLU, g of z is equal to max of 0, z.

00:05:58.020 --> 00:06:09.500
So the derivative is equal to, turns out to be 0 if z is less than 0 and 1 if z is greater than 0.

00:06:09.500 --> 00:06:16.620
And it's actually a technically undefined if z is equal to exactly 0.

00:06:16.620 --> 00:06:22.020
But if you're implementing this in software, it might not be 100% mathematically correct,

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:29.060
but it'll work just fine if z is exactly 0 if you set the derivative to be equal to 1,

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:32.220
or if you set it to be 0, it kind of doesn't matter.

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:37.460
If you're on exponent optimization, technically, g prime then becomes what's called a sub-gradient

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:42.180
of the activation function g of z, which is why gradient descent still works.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:48.060
But you can think of it as that, the chance of z being 0. exactly 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0,

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.820
is so small that it almost doesn't matter what you set the derivative to be equal to

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:54.260
when z is equal to 0.

00:06:54.260 --> 00:06:59.220
So in practice, this is what people implement for the derivative of z.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:04.940
And finally, if you are training on your network with the Leakey ReLU activation function,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:14.100
then g of z is going to be max of, say, 0.01z, z.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:26.740
And so g prime of z is equal to 0.01 if z is less than 0 and 1 if z is greater than 0.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:31.220
And once again, the gradient is technically not defined when z is exactly equal to 0.

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:36.100
But if you implement a piece of code that sets the derivative or that sets g prime to

00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:41.780
either 0.01 or 0.01, either way, it doesn't really matter when z is exactly 0, your code

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:42.980
will work just fine.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.660
So onto these formulas, you should really compute the slopes or the derivatives of your

00:07:47.660 --> 00:07:49.900
activation functions.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:54.180
Now that you have this building block, you're ready to see how to implement gradient descent

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:55.180
for your neural network.

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:57.220
Let's go on to the next video to see that.
